---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import Hero from '../components/Hero.astro';
import ContentSection from '../components/ContentSection.astro';
import LinkCard from '../components/LinkCard.astro';

const relatedPages = [
  { title: 'Common Mistakes', href: '/common-betting-mistakes', description: 'Avoiding errors' },
  { title: 'Big Games', href: '/why-big-games-attract-bad-bets', description: 'Event psychology' },
  { title: 'Betting Strategy', href: '/betting-strategy', description: 'Decision framework' },
];
---

<Layout
  title="The Role of Media Narratives in Betting Lines"
  description="How sports media narratives influence public betting behavior and whether they affect betting line accuracy."
>
  <Hero
    title="Media Narratives and Betting Lines"
    subtitle="How Storylines Influence Markets and Bettors"
    size="medium"
  />

  <div class="container content-container">
    <article class="page-content">
      <ContentSection>
        <div class="quick-take">
          <h2>Quick Take</h2>
          <p>
            Sports media produces compelling narratives—revenge games, legacy moments, team-of-destiny stories—that captivate audiences but have limited predictive value. These narratives influence casual bettors, potentially moving lines toward popular sides. Understanding the difference between entertaining storylines and analytical factors helps you avoid narrative-driven betting while recognizing when public bias might create opportunity.
          </p>
        </div>
      </ContentSection>

      <ContentSection title="How Do Narratives Influence Betting?" id="influence">
        <h3>Narratives drive casual betting</h3>
        <p>
          Stories about "Brady's revenge tour" or "Curry's legacy game" encourage betting based on storyline rather than matchup analysis.
        </p>

        <h3>Narratives are entertainment, not analysis</h3>
        <p>
          Media narratives exist to engage viewers, not to predict outcomes. They're optimized for compelling content, not accuracy.
        </p>

        <h3>Public money follows narratives</h3>
        <p>
          When narratives align with existing public biases (toward favorites, popular teams), they can amplify betting imbalances.
        </p>
      </ContentSection>

      <ContentSection title="What This Means for Bettors" id="what-means">
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Separate entertainment from analysis:</strong> Enjoy the storylines; don't bet on them.</li>
          <li><strong>Recognize narrative-driven line movement:</strong> Lines moving toward story-driven sides might offer value on the other side—but only if your analysis supports it.</li>
          <li><strong>Trust data over stories:</strong> Matchups, metrics, and situational factors outpredict narratives.</li>
        </ul>
      </ContentSection>

      <ContentSection title="Key Takeaways" id="takeaways">
        <ul>
          <li>Media narratives are entertainment, not predictive tools</li>
          <li>Casual bettors often follow storylines</li>
          <li>Narrative-driven public betting can create market imbalances</li>
          <li>Your analysis should be based on data, not stories</li>
        </ul>
      </ContentSection>

      <ContentSection title="Related Resources" id="related">
        <div class="related-grid">
          {relatedPages.map((page) => (
            <LinkCard title={page.title} href={page.href} description={page.description} />
          ))}
        </div>
      </ContentSection>
    </article>
  </div>
</Layout>

<style>
  .page-content { padding: var(--spacing-3xl) 0; }
  .quick-take { background-color: var(--color-surface); border-left: 4px solid var(--color-accent); padding: var(--spacing-lg); border-radius: 0 var(--border-radius) var(--border-radius) 0; margin-bottom: var(--spacing-xl); }
  .quick-take h2 { font-size: 1rem; text-transform: uppercase; letter-spacing: 0.05em; color: var(--color-accent); margin-bottom: var(--spacing-sm); }
  .quick-take p { margin: 0; color: var(--color-text-secondary); }
  .related-grid { display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(3, 1fr); gap: var(--spacing-md); margin-top: var(--spacing-lg); }
  @media (max-width: 768px) { .related-grid { grid-template-columns: 1fr; } }
</style>
